---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Days between dates: count days and working days between two dates"
  description="Count the days between any two dates, and the working days once weekends are taken out. Useful for deadlines, notice periods and countdowns."
>
  <h1>Days between dates</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Two dates, one subtraction. This page counts the calendar days between them, and, if you
    tick the box, only the Monday to Friday days in between.
  </p>

  <div class="sheet">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="start">First date</label>
        <input type="date" id="start" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="end">Second date</label>
        <input type="date" id="end" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <label style="display:inline-flex;gap:0.5rem;align-items:center;color:var(--color-ink-2);">
        <input type="checkbox" id="workdays" /> Count working days only (Monday to Friday)
      </label>
    </p>
    <button class="btn btn--primary" id="calc" type="button">Count the days</button>
    <div id="out" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p class="result-note">Choose two dates and the count appears here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What counts as a day here</h2>
  <p>
    The count is the number of whole days you have to wait from the first date to reach the
    second one. It does not count the starting day itself. So 1 January to 2 January is one
    day, not two. That is the way notice periods, rental agreements and most deadlines are
    written, which is why this page counts that way.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you need the count to include both the first and the last day, add one to the answer.
    Plenty of people need it that way for hotel nights and holiday bookings, and neither way
    is wrong, they are just different questions.
  </p>

  <h2>One example, worked by hand</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Span</th><th scope="col">Working</th><th scope="col" class="num">Days</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 January 2026 to 13 November 2026</td><td>Day 1 of the year to day 317</td><td class="num">316</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Same span, weekends removed</td><td>45 full weeks, plus the extra days</td><td class="num">226</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>1 January 2026 to 1 January 2027</td><td>A plain non-leap year</td><td class="num">365</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>Does it count the first day?</dt>
    <dd>
      No. It counts the days between the two dates, so the first day is day zero. Add one to
      the answer if your form or booking counts both ends.
    </dd>
    <dt>What about public holidays?</dt>
    <dd>
      The working-days count removes Saturdays and Sundays only. Holidays differ too much by
      country and state to bake into one page, so check your local calendar for those.
    </dd>
    <dt>Can the second date be earlier than the first?</dt>
    <dd>Yes. The page just swaps them and counts the same span. The answer is always positive.</dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Method: whole calendar days between the two dates, counted on the UTC calendar so daylight saving never adds or loses a day. Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

  <script>
    const sEl = document.getElementById('start');
    const eEl = document.getElementById('end');
    const wEl = document.getElementById('workdays');
    const out = document.getElementById('out');

    function compute() {
      const s = sEl.valueAsDate;
      const e = eEl.valueAsDate;
      if (!s || !e) {
        out.innerHTML = '<p class="result-note">Choose two dates and the count appears here.</p>';
        return;
      }
      const a = Date.UTC(s.getFullYear(), s.getMonth(), s.getDate());
      const b = Date.UTC(e.getFullYear(), e.getMonth(), e.getDate());
      const lo = Math.min(a, b);
      const hi = Math.max(a, b);
      const days = Math.round((hi - lo) / 86400000);
      const fmt = (n) => n.toLocaleString('en-GB');
      if (!wEl.checked) {
        const weeks = Math.floor(days / 7);
        const rem = days % 7;
        out.innerHTML =
          '<p class="result-figure">' + fmt(days) + ' days</p>' +
          '<p>That is ' + fmt(weeks) + ' full week' + (weeks === 1 ? '' : 's') + ' and ' + rem + ' extra day' + (rem === 1 ? '' : 's') + ', or ' +
          fmt(days * 24) + ' hours if you are counting down to something.</p>';
        return;
      }
      let work = 0;
      for (let t = lo + 86400000; t <= hi; t += 86400000) {
        const dow = new Date(t).getUTCDay();
        if (dow >= 1 && dow <= 5) work += 1;
      }
      out.innerHTML =
        '<p class="result-figure">' + fmt(work) + ' working days</p>' +
        '<p>The full span is ' + fmt(days) + ' calendar days, of which ' + fmt(work) +
        ' fall on a Monday to Friday. Public holidays are not taken out, check your local calendar for those.</p>';
    }

    document.getElementById('calc').addEventListener('click', compute);
    sEl.addEventListener('change', compute);
    eEl.addEventListener('change', compute);
    wEl.addEventListener('change', compute);
  </script>
</Base>
